      "info": {
        "title": "IRIS – tube inspection from the inside",
        "description": "IRIS (Internal Rotary Inspection System) is an ultrasonic method for inspecting tubes from the inside. A probe with a rotating mirror, driven by water flow, scans the full circumference as it is pulled through the tube.\n\nIt measures the remaining wall thickness and finds corrosion, erosion, pitting and other wall loss on both the inner and the outer surface.",
        "why": "Accurate wall-thickness data for every tube in boilers, heat exchangers and air coolers (fin-fans), so you plug or replace only the tubes that need it and plan shutdowns with confidence.",
        "faq": [
          {
            "q": "Which equipment is IRIS used for?",
            "a": "Tubes in heat exchangers, boilers and air coolers (fin-fans), in both ferrous and non-ferrous materials."
          },
          {
            "q": "Do the tubes need to be cleaned first?",
            "a": "Yes. IRIS needs clean tubes filled with water, so tubes are cleaned before inspection to get accurate readings."
          },
          {
            "q": "How does it compare with eddy current testing?",
            "a": "IRIS measures the remaining wall thickness very accurately and shows exactly where the loss is. It is often used together with faster screening methods such as eddy current or RFET, to confirm and size what they find."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
